#: ../../../intro/01_first_figure.rst:22
msgid "1. Making your first figure"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../intro/01_first_figure.rst:24
msgid ""
"This tutorial covers the basics of creating a figure using PyGMT - a Python "
"wrapper for the Generic Mapping Tools (GMT). It will only use the :meth:"
"`pygmt.Figure.coast` method for plotting. Later tutorials will address other "
"PyGMT methods."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../intro/01_first_figure.rst:32
msgid "Loading the library"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../intro/01_first_figure.rst:34
msgid ""
"The first step is to import :mod:`pygmt`. All methods and figure generation "
"are accessible from the :mod:`pygmt` top level package."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../intro/01_first_figure.rst:39
msgid ""
"import pygmt\n"
"from pygmt.params import Frame"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../intro/01_first_figure.rst:49
msgid "Creating a figure"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../intro/01_first_figure.rst:51
msgid ""
"All figure generation in PyGMT is handled by the :class:`pygmt.Figure` "
"class. Start a new figure by creating an instance of this class:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../intro/01_first_figure.rst:56
msgid "fig = pygmt.Figure()"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../intro/01_first_figure.rst:64
msgid ""
"To add elements to the figure instance or object (``fig`` in this example) "
"different methods can be called on it. This example will use the :meth:"
"`pygmt.Figure.coast` method, which can be used to create a map without any "
"other methods or external data. The :meth:`pygmt.Figure.coast` method plots "
"the coastlines, borders, and bodies of water using a database that is "
"included in GMT."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../intro/01_first_figure.rst:71
msgid ""
"First, a region for the figure must be selected. This example will plot some "
"of the coast of Maine in the northeastern US. A Python list can be passed to "
"the ``region`` parameter with the minimum and maximum X-values (longitude) "
"and the minimum and maximum Y-values (latitude). For this example, the "
"minimum (bottom left) coordinates are (N43.75, W69) and the maximum (top "
"right) coordinates are (N44.75, W68). Negative values can be passed for "
"latitudes in the southern hemisphere or longitudes in the western hemisphere."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../intro/01_first_figure.rst:79
msgid ""
"In addition to the region, an argument needs to be passed to :meth:`pygmt."
"Figure.coast` to tell it what to plot. In this example, :meth:`pygmt.Figure."
"coast` will be told to plot the shorelines by passing the Boolean value "
"``True`` to the ``shorelines`` parameter. The ``shorelines`` parameter has "
"other options for finer control, but setting it to ``True`` uses the default "
"values."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../intro/01_first_figure.rst:88
msgid "fig.coast(region=[-69, -68, 43.75, 44.75], shorelines=True)"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../intro/01_first_figure.rst:96
msgid "To see the figure, call :meth:`pygmt.Figure.show`."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../intro/01_first_figure.rst:100
msgid "fig.show()"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../intro/01_first_figure.rst:110
msgid "Color the land and water"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../intro/01_first_figure.rst:112
msgid ""
"This figure plots all of the coastlines in the given region, but it does not "
"indicate where the land and water are. Color values can be passed to "
"``land`` and ``water`` to set the colors on the figure."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../intro/01_first_figure.rst:116
msgid ""
"When plotting colors in PyGMT, there are multiple :gmt-docs:`color codes "
"<gmtcolors.html>`, that can be used. This includes standard GMT color names "
"(like ``\"skyblue\"``), R/G/B levels (like ``\"0/0/255\"``), a hex value "
"(like ``\"#333333\"``), or a gray level (like ``\"gray50\"``). For this "
"example, GMT color names are used."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../intro/01_first_figure.rst:124
msgid ""
"fig = pygmt.Figure()\n"
"fig.coast(\n"
"    region=[-69, -68, 43.75, 44.75],\n"
"    shorelines=True,\n"
"    land=\"lightgreen\",\n"
"    water=\"lightblue\",\n"
")\n"
"fig.show()"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../intro/01_first_figure.rst:141
msgid "Set the projection"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../intro/01_first_figure.rst:143
msgid ""
"This figure now has its colors set. By default the projection and size of "
"the map is set to ``\"Q15c+du\"``. Here, ``\"Q\"`` defines a cylindrical "
"equidistant map projection, ``\"15c+du\"`` means setting the maximum (upper) "
"map dimension to 15 cm. However, both of these values can be customized "
"according to the requirements using the ``projection`` parameter."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../intro/01_first_figure.rst:150
msgid ""
"The appropriate projection varies for the type of map. The available "
"projections are explained in the :doc:`projection </projections/index>` "
"gallery. For this example, the Mercator projection is set using ``\"M\"``. "
"The width of the figure will be 10 centimeters, as set by ``\"10c\"``. The "
"map size can also be set in inches using \"i\" (e.g. a 5-inch wide Mercator "
"projection would use ``\"M5i\"``)."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../intro/01_first_figure.rst:159
msgid ""
"fig = pygmt.Figure()\n"
"fig.coast(\n"
"    region=[-69, -68, 43.75, 44.75],\n"
"    shorelines=True,\n"
"    land=\"lightgreen\",\n"
"    water=\"lightblue\",\n"
"    projection=\"M10c\",\n"
")\n"
"fig.show()"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../intro/01_first_figure.rst:177
msgid "Add a frame"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../intro/01_first_figure.rst:179
msgid ""
"While the map's colors, projection, and size have been set, the region that "
"is being displayed is not apparent. A frame can be added to annotate the "
"latitude and longitude of the region."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../intro/01_first_figure.rst:183
msgid ""
"The ``frame`` parameter is used to add a frame to the figure. For now, it "
"will be set to ``True`` to annotate the axes automatically."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../intro/01_first_figure.rst:188
msgid ""
"fig = pygmt.Figure()\n"
"fig.coast(\n"
"    region=[-69, -68, 43.75, 44.75],\n"
"    shorelines=True,\n"
"    land=\"lightgreen\",\n"
"    water=\"lightblue\",\n"
"    projection=\"M10c\",\n"
"    frame=True,\n"
")\n"
"fig.show()"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../intro/01_first_figure.rst:207
msgid "Add a title"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../intro/01_first_figure.rst:209
msgid ""
"The ``frame`` parameter can also be used to add a title to the figure. "
"Here, :class:`pygmt.params.Frame` sets the map title."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../intro/01_first_figure.rst:214
msgid ""
"fig = pygmt.Figure()\n"
"fig.coast(\n"
"    region=[-69, -68, 43.75, 44.75],\n"
"    shorelines=True,\n"
"    land=\"lightgreen\",\n"
"    water=\"lightblue\",\n"
"    projection=\"M10c\",\n"
"    frame=Frame(title=\"Maine\"),\n"
")\n"
"fig.show()"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../intro/01_first_figure.rst:233
msgid "Additional exercises"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../intro/01_first_figure.rst:235
msgid ""
"This is the end of the first tutorial. Here are some additional exercises "
"for the concepts that were discussed:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../intro/01_first_figure.rst:238
msgid ""
"Make a map of Germany using its ISO country code (\"DE\"). Pass the ISO code "
"as a Python string to the ``region`` parameter."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../intro/01_first_figure.rst:241
msgid ""
"Change the color of the landmass to \"khaki\" and the water to \"azure\"."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../intro/01_first_figure.rst:243
msgid ""
"Change the color of the lakes (using the ``lakes`` parameter) to \"red\"."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../intro/01_first_figure.rst:245
msgid ""
"Create a global map. Set the region to \"d\" to center the map at the Prime "
"Meridian or \"g\" to center the map at the International Date Line. When the "
"region is set without using a list full of integers or floating numbers, the "
"argument needs to be passed as a Python string. Create a map with a width of "
"15 centimeters using the Mollweide (\"W\") projection."
msgstr ""

